{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the streets like confetti. I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, each one a quiet prayer etched in wood. No crowds, just the whisper of wind and the occasional bell from a hidden shrine.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed with neon and precision. I ate ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, slurping broth so rich it felt like warmth in my chest. The cashier bowed. I bowed back. No words needed.\n\nI took the Shinkansen to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was still. A single origami crane, left by a child, sat on the monument’s edge. I bought one myself and placed it beside it.\n\nIn Nara, deer bowed—not literally, but they knew where the snacks were. I fed them gently, amazed at how calmly they waited, how gently they licked my palm. They didn’t rush. They didn’t demand. Just presence.\n\nAt a ryokan in Hakone, I soaked in an onsen under a sky full of stars. The water was hot. The silence, deeper. My host, an elderly woman with silver hair, served matcha in a bowl so delicate it felt like holding a cloud.\n\nJapan didn’t overwhelm me with spectacle. It whispered. It waited. It offered tea, not tickets. I learned to pause—to savor the space between breaths, the quiet before the next train, the space between “thank you” and “it was perfect.”\n\nI didn’t bring back many souvenirs. Just a few dried sakura leaves, a worn notebook, and the quiet understanding that beauty doesn’t need to shout.\n\nI left with more than photos. I left with stillness.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"